# Paper flow — v2, 2026-09-11
**Every number below was recomputed from the result files on 2026-09-11.** Claims that failed
verification are marked ✗ and replaced. Provenance for each is in `FINDINGS.md`.

**Working title:** *Where You Look Is Worth More Than How Much You See: Budget-Matched Evidence
That Query-Independent Token Allocation Is the Bottleneck in VLMs*

**Thesis.** Production VLMs spend their visual token budget uniformly over space and independently
of the question. At matched *realized* token budget this costs more than 26x the compute on
localizable-evidence questions; the evidence still reaches the readout; and the model does not use it.

---

## §1 Introduction

**The gap.** Every shipped high-resolution pathway — LLaVA-NeXT AnyRes, InternVL2 dynamic tiling,
Qwen dynamic resolution — makes the same choice: spend a large budget, laid out **uniformly over
space**, with the layout fixed **before the question is read**. That choice has never been priced,
because the natural comparison has only been run at matched image *area*, never at matched *token
budget*.

**Contributions, in the order the paper earns them.**
1. **A budget-matched protocol on realized tokens, and evidence it is not a technicality (§2).**
   Applying the gate **per item** rather than to the median changed this paper's headline from
   **+33.5pp to +25.9pp** — a 23% relative reduction — because a median-based gate reported 0.0%
   spread on a run where **39.3% of items were individually mismatched** (p90 46.4%, max 115.2%).
   The direction of a mismatch is also **not predictable in advance**: in one earlier experiment it
   *favoured* the arm under test by 19%, while in three rejected builds of the experiment reported
   here it *handicapped* the winning arm (which received 1176-1488 tokens against the baseline's
   2144). Those builds therefore understated rather than inflated the effect — but that can only be
   asserted *after* running the gated version. Reporting realized tokens per arm is what converts an
   unknown-sign bias into a measured one.
2. **The operative variable is placement, not budget (§3).** At matched tokens, on the architecture
   that introduced AnyRes: query placement beats the shipped pathway by **+25.9pp**, and the same
   budget spent on a *random* region is **12.9pp worse than tiling**.
3. **No crossing at 26x budget (§4).** Query-independent spending is still significantly behind a
   300-token query-placed crop at 7957 realized tokens — **and we report where this fails**: on
   relative-position questions the gap closes to insignificance.
4. **The regime is sharply bounded (§5):** error rate **32.8% -> 1.0%** across target token budget.
5. **A mechanism (§6):** the evidence is present at the answer position and unused — a *use*
   failure, not a routing or capacity failure.
6. **Boundaries, and five hypotheses of our own that died to their own controls (§7).**

**Not claimed.** Oracle regions throughout; no localizer proposed; no comparison with V*/SEAL. §9.

---

## §2 Method: matched realized budget

**Protocol.** Each arm is calibrated by iterating on the processor's own `image_grid_thw` until
realized merged-token counts agree, then verified by a **per-item budget gate**: an item enters a
contrast only if its own arms agree within 10%. Items failing the gate are dropped and counted.

**Why analytic budgets fail.** Solving token count from pixel geometry is overridden by the
processor's internal resize. In an early build, requesting 150 tokens gave the single-image arm 120
and the two-image arms 143 — a **19% budget advantage to the arm under test**.

**Two measured architectural constraints.**
* **Qwen3-VL:** every image costs **>=64 merged tokens** regardless of size (28x28 px -> 64;
  112x112 px -> 64). A 2x2 grid plus thumbnail therefore cannot cost below 320 tokens.
* **LLaVA-NeXT:** the count is a **coarse step function** of input size (it snaps to an
  `image_grid_pinpoints` entry), and the reachable values depend on **aspect ratio** — measured on
  real V*Bench images, `anyres` realized **21 distinct counts spanning 1752-2634** (124/191 at
  2144). It also **unpads tile features by aspect ratio**, so a region whose aspect differs from the
  source can never realize the source's count at any scale.
  *(✗ An earlier draft said "only 1416 or 2242 are realizable." That came from a synthetic probe at
  one fixed image size and is false for real images.)*
⇒ **The tiling pathway has a minimum budget.** It cannot be run cheaply at all.

**Table 1 (the receipts).** Void builds compared **on their own items**, with the admissible build
restricted to those same items. The void builds died early (n=52, n=57) and V*Bench is ordered with
easier `direct_attributes` questions first, so raw cross-build percentages are **not** comparable
and are not used.

| build | n | tokens: `alloc_query` vs `anyres` | spread | `alloc_query` | admissible, SAME items | `anyres` | same items |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| v1 proportional solver / global budget axis | 52 | **1176** vs 2144 | 82.3% | 96.2% | **94.2%** | 50.0% | **48.1%** |
| v2 ladder, no aspect match | 57 | **1488** vs 2144 | 44.1% | 96.5% | **94.7%** | 49.1% | **49.1%** |
| **v3 ladder + aspect match (used)** | 191 | 2144 vs 2144 | per-item gated | 86.2% (n=116) | — | 60.3% | — |

**How to read Table 1 — and what it does NOT show.** On identical items the void and admissible
builds agree to within ~2pp, so these particular void builds were *approximately right*, not
artifacts. The reason is visible in the token columns: in every one of them `alloc_query` received
**fewer** tokens than `anyres` (1176-1488 vs 2144), so the mismatch **handicapped the arm that won**
and the reported effect was conservative.

That is the honest lesson, and it is not "the gate saved us here." It is: **the sign of a budget
mismatch is not predictable from the design.** In Phase 17 an analytic budget solve handed the arm
under test a **19% advantage** (which would have inflated the result); here the same class of error
produced a **45-83% disadvantage** to the same kind of arm. Nothing about the experiment's shape
told us which we had. Measuring realized tokens is what turns an unknown-sign bias into a known one,
and the per-item gate is what stops matched and unmatched items being averaged into a single
number.

**The gate must be per item.** On the final run medians agreed exactly (0.0% spread) while
**39.3% of items were mismatched by >10%** (per-item spread p90 = 46.4%, max = 115.2%).
*(✗ The first version of §3 reported all 191 items under a median gate. Corrected below.)*

**Figure 1.** Per-item realized-token spread, ECDF, with the 10% gate line; dropped fraction labelled.

---

## §3 Result 1 — the operative variable is placement, not budget

**Setup.** LLaVA-NeXT-vicuna-7b, V*Bench, 4-way MCQ (argmax over {A,B,C,D} logits — a readout
intrinsically immune to the bias-shift artifact that inflates yes/no metrics). Every arm is a
**single image** through `LlavaNextProcessor` unmodified: no arm differs in image count or format.
Each item matched to **its own native AnyRes cost**. Reported on the **per-item matched subset,
n=116 of 191** (75 dropped, §2).

| arm | accuracy (n=116) |
|---|---|
| uniform (cheapest whole-image; unmatched reference) | 50.0% |
| **anyres** — the shipped high-resolution path | 60.3% |
| **alloc_query** — same budget, the question's region | **86.2%** |
| **alloc_random** — same budget, size-matched wrong region | 47.4% |

| contrast (paired, per-item matched) | delta |
|---|---|
| query placement vs native AnyRes | **+25.9pp** [+17.2, +34.5] |
| query vs size-matched random (decider) | **+38.8pp** [+29.3, +48.3] |
| **random sub-region vs tiling** | **-12.9pp** [-22.4, -4.3] **reversed** |

*(Under the looser median gate over all 191 these read +33.5 / +48.7 / -15.2pp. All three
conclusions survive the stricter gate; all three shrink. We report the stricter numbers.)*

**Figure 2.** Four bars with CIs; the reversed contrast annotated.

**The third row is the section.** A placement-free sub-region at the identical budget *loses* to
tiling. So this is not "cropping beats tiling" and not "sub-regions beat whole images." The budget
is not the constraint; the placement policy is.

**By question type (n=116):** `direct_attributes` **+32.1pp** [+20.8, +45.3] (n=53);
`relative_position` **+20.6pp** [+9.5, +31.7] (n=63). Both significant; the effect is larger where
evidence is single-region. This foreshadows §4's failure case.

**Supporting, at matched image count (Qwen3-VL, appendix):** a tight crop beats a **grid-aligned
tile containing the same target** by +27.2pp (B=600) and +18.8pp (B=1200). Grid alignment is itself
expensive — a constraint on any tiling method, tested by neither prior system, since both select
free-form boxes.

**Stated limitation of the arm.** Because aspect matching is required (§2), `alloc_query` here is
*the query region expanded to source aspect ratio*, not a tight crop. It is **not** a tight-crop
upper bound.

---

## §4 Result 2 — the asymptote: no crossing at 26x

**Setup.** Qwen3-VL-2B (chosen because its budget knob is *continuous*; LLaVA-NeXT's step function
cannot sweep). `crop_only` held **fixed** at 300 tokens; `uniform` swept over a measured ladder.
**Both arms single image** — format-clean at every rung. Per-item realization is tight here
(|off| p90 <= 4% on every rung), so no items are dropped. n=191.

Reference: `crop_only@300` = **93.7%** [89.3, 96.4]. `crop_random@300` = **38.2%** — same budget,
wrong place, and *below* `uniform` at the same 300 tokens (56.5%).

| uniform, realized tokens | accuracy | vs `crop_only@300` |
|---|---|---|
| 150 | 46.6% | -47.1pp [-54.5, -39.3] |
| 294 | 56.5% | -37.2pp [-45.0, -29.3] |
| 600 | 66.0% | -27.7pp [-35.1, -20.4] |
| 1176 | 70.2% | -23.6pp [-30.9, -16.2] |
| 2400 | 75.4% | -18.3pp [-25.1, -11.0] |
| 4760 | 81.2% | -12.6pp [-19.4, -6.3] |
| **7957** | **84.8%** | **-8.9pp [-15.2, -2.6]** |

**Figure 3 (headline).** Accuracy vs realized tokens, log-x; `crop_only@300` as a horizontal line;
shaded gap; last rung annotated *"26x the budget, still 8.9pp behind."*

**Stated as a bound.** No crossing up to 7957 realized tokens = **26.5x** the placed budget.
Robustness: restricting to the 189 items whose `crop_only` realized within 10% of 300 gives
-8.5pp [-14.8, -2.6] — unchanged. The gap closes ~3.7pp per doubling in the last rungs, so parity
by extrapolation lies beyond ~40k tokens; **that is extrapolation, not a measured result.**

### §4.1 Where this fails — and it is the honest half of the section
Split by question type:

| category | n | `crop_only@300` | `uniform@7957` | gap |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `direct_attributes` | 115 | 97.4% | 85.2% | **-12.2pp [-19.1, -5.2]** significant |
| `relative_position` | 76 | 88.2% | 84.2% | **-3.9pp [-15.8, +6.6]** **spans zero** |

⇒ **The no-crossing result holds for single-region questions and does NOT hold for
relative-position questions**, where enough uniform budget does catch up. This is the boundary that
makes the claim scientific: *query-independent budget fails to substitute for placement when the
evidence is localizable, and substitutes adequately when the question needs multiple regions.*

### §4.2 The 1/area scaling law is NOT supported — reported as a negative
All three target-size terciles fail to cross by 7957 tokens (smallest 83% vs 90.5%; largest 86% vs
96.9%), because V*Bench's *largest* targets are still tiny (median area fraction 0.001). The
benchmark occupies one regime, so the size-dependence of the crossing budget cannot be resolved
here. Testing the exponent needs a wide target-size range — RePOPE-clean spans 0.08 to 290 merged
tokens — and is left open.

---

## §5 Result 3 — the regime is sharply bounded

RePOPE-clean positives, n=3387; error rate by merged tokens on the target, computed from stored
`image_grid_thw` plus COCO boxes (no additional inference).

| tokens on object | n | error rate |
|---|---|---|
| [0, 0.5) | 174 | **32.8%** |
| [0.5, 1) | 129 | 18.6% |
| [1, 2) | 189 | 7.9% |
| [2, 4) | 219 | 5.5% |
| [4, 8) | 252 | 4.8% |
| [8, 32) | 678 | 2.2% |
| [32, inf) | 1746 | **1.0%** |

**Figure 4.** Error rate vs tokens-on-object, log-x, monotone across seven strata — a **33x** swing
on a benchmark that is 95.5% accurate in aggregate. *The aggregate hides the entire phenomenon.*

This defines where the allocation effect can bind, and is why V*Bench (median area fraction 0.001)
is the right venue while POPE's aggregate is not.

---

## §6 Result 4 — mechanism: a *use* failure

**Eliminations, each with its own control.**
* **Not a capacity floor.** A linear probe recovers the target from hidden states.
* **Not misdirected attention.** Attention-guided top-k retention keeps the target **+20 to +35pp
  above chance** at L2/L4/L8. *This refuted our own draft claim that "there is nothing at that
  location to attend to."*
* **Not fixable by pointing.** A box drawn on the pixels recovers 5.6%; normalized coordinates in
  the prompt, 0.0%; a "focus on X" instruction, 0.0%.
* **Not fixable by one-layer steering.** L16 steering shows no real recovery once the matched
  random-direction control is applied.
* **Not miscalibration.** Retuning the decision threshold per size stratum buys **0.2pp**
  (93.7% -> 93.9%), because negatives sit at **median p_yes = 0.0000** and the class distributions
  barely overlap. *This kills the most obvious reviewer alternative in one line.*

**The positive result — answer-matched probe.** Condition on the model's answer, then probe the
truth inside it. **ANSWERED-NO cohort, n=612**: 153 objects actually present (false negatives) vs
459 actually absent (true negatives). The model said "no" to all 612, so its answer is **constant
across both probe classes** and decoding it yields nothing.

| | AUROC |
|---|---|
| permutation null (label-shuffled, same CV) | 0.531 |
| baseline: `p_yes` alone, a single scalar | 0.899 |
| `last_blank` (same position, blank image = language prior) | 0.70 - 0.83 |
| **`last` (answer position)** | **0.958 (L8) -> 0.982 (L16) -> 0.977 (L28)** |

**Figure 5.** AUROC vs layer for `last` and `last_blank`, with the `p_yes` baseline and permutation
null as horizontal lines.

⇒ **Graded evidence survives to the readout and is not thresholded into the answer.** Same picture
in the sub-token stratum (n=555, 96 present / 459 absent; baseline 0.870, null 0.575).

**Caveats printed with the result.** `last_blank` at 0.70-0.83 is high — within a "said no" cohort
the question text alone predicts a lot — so the claim rests on the **gap**, not on 0.98 absolute.
And `p_yes` alone already carries 0.899, so the defensible phrasing is *"graded evidence is not
used"*, not *"the model secretly knows."*

---

## §7 Boundaries, negative results, and our own retractions

A section, not a footnote. Five of our hypotheses died to their own controls:
1. **"VLMs localize well but answer poorly"** — killed; P(yes) AUROC **0.9725** on POPE.
2. **"Answer-by-grounding as a detector"** — killed; the grounding channel emits a box on 100% of
   P(yes)'s false positives.
3. **"Re-anchoring on the model's own failed percept"** — +17.0pp on raw recovery, died on its
   false-positive control (discrimination CI [-10.6, +6.4]).
4. **"Attention pruning drops small targets"** — refuted; we had conflated attention *mass* with
   attention *rank*.
5. **"Presence information arrives at L6-L9"** — **retracted the same day**. The `last` probe was
   circular: it decodes the model's own answer. Proven by a pre-registered diagnostic — cohorts with
   *opposite* label-answer signs both reached AUROC 1.000.

**Plus one methodological self-correction (§2, §3):** our own budget gate was median-based and
passed a run in which 39.3% of items were individually mismatched.

**Scope limits.** CUB fine-grained over-acceptance is **not** explained by allocation (2.2%, a
different failure mode). Relational questions benefit less (§3) and lose the no-crossing property
entirely (§4.1).

**Orthogonal finding.** Confidence-selected cohorts are **7.2x enriched** for benchmark label errors
— a warning for anyone selecting "confident failures" on POPE.

---

## §8 Related work

**Cite for the phenomenon, which is theirs.** Q-CueGraph (2608.04452): V*Bench 0.696 full-image ->
0.833, with an area-matched anti-region control at 0.393 and a shuffled-question control at 0.618.
RUTA (2608.04132): a random-region control on Qwen3-VL. **Our `alloc_random` at 38-47% lands on
their anti-region number — a healthy external replication, and we say so.**

**What is ours.** (i) matching on **realized tokens** rather than image area — Q-CueGraph matches
area; RUTA states outright its localizer comparison is "not a strictly rate-matched ranking";
(ii) a **native-tiling arm** at that matched budget, which neither runs; (iii) the **asymptote and
its failure case** (§4, §4.1), which only a rate-matched protocol can ask; (iv) the **use-failure
mechanism** (§6), since neither paper touches internals. Q-CueGraph's §4.4 (14% of crops containing
the gold answer still yield a wrong answer) is the *behavioural* shadow of our §6 and is cited as
such.

---

## §9 Limitations

Oracle regions throughout — every `alloc_query` arm uses ground-truth boxes, stated as an oracle.
No localizer proposed; a training-free proposer would be reported as *% of oracle gain captured*,
not as a method. We do not compete with V*/SEAL. §6 is one model. §3's `alloc_query` is aspect-
expanded, not a tight crop. The 1/area scaling law is unsupported (§4.2). §4's no-crossing bound is
category-dependent (§4.1).

---

## Missing before submission (ranked)

1. **RQ-C — is the exchange rate architecture-invariant?** Run §4's sweep on tokenizers that
   disagree about everything (Qwen3-VL 16px dynamic; LLaVA-NeXT 336px AnyRes; Qwen2-VL 14px;
   llava-onevision 384px). Roughly constant => a property of the **task**. Wildly varying => the
   claim narrows to a survey, honestly.
2. **RQ-D — where does placement HURT?** §4.1 says relative-position is the place to look.
   Pre-registered admissibility: uniform accuracy must be <85% or the test is void.
3. **RQ-B on RePOPE** — the scaling exponent, on the only benchmark with the target-size range.
4. **§6 on a second model.**
5. Re-run §3's per-item gate over the dropped 75 items with tighter aspect matching, to recover
   power.

