# METHOD SECTION (added 2026-09-11) — the paper was all measurement, no method. This fixes it.

## §2b The primitive: budget-preserving spatial reallocation

Every prior comparison in this space varies the token budget while varying the strategy. We hold
the **realized** token budget fixed and vary only *where* it is spent. `fit_to_budget` is the
implementation: iterate on the processor's own `image_grid_thw` until the realized merged-token
count matches a target, then compare.

**What is genuinely new here is narrower than "a new method," and §8 must say so plainly:**
> We do not propose a better cropper. ViCrop already crops from attention and re-encodes. Our delta
> is the **budget-preserving constraint** — ViCrop's crop costs whatever it costs; ours costs
> exactly B — plus the demonstration that **the token budget, held fixed, is the axis on which
> cropping and pruning should have been compared all along.**

## §6b The method claim: allocation vs pruning

The two families that address this problem move on different axes:

| family | what it does | encoder cost | LLM cost |
|---|---|---|---|
| **pruning** (FastV, D2Pruner, RoRA, HoloV, VisionZip) | selects which already-encoded tokens reach the LLM | **full** | reduced to B |
| **allocation** (ours) | changes what the encoder samples | reduced to B | reduced to B |

> **Pruning selects among a fixed spatial sampling. Allocation changes the sampling.**

A pruner cannot spend more resolution on a region than the encoder's grid already gave it.

**Prediction with a mechanism, and a crossing point.** In the sub-token regime the target occupies
<1 merged token *at the encoder's grid*, so **no subset of those tokens carries it**, whichever
subset the pruner keeps. Above ~2-4 tokens the pruner has something to select and the gap should
close. §5's dose-response curve supplies the x-axis.

**How we make this comparison — and what we could NOT do.** We attempted a faithful FastV
reimplementation on Qwen3-VL (attention rank at an early layer, keep top-k, re-forward on the
pruned sequence). **It is blocked by the backbone**: Qwen3-VL injects *deepstack* visual features
at several LLM layers, so `get_image_features` returns 4x as many feature rows as visual token
positions (560 rows of dim 1024 for 140 positions at hidden 2048). Subsetting the main sequence
without subsetting the deepstack injections identically produces a silently wrong model. We judged
the risk of a subtly incorrect baseline higher than the value of the row, and did not ship it.
*(Recorded so this reads as a decision, not an omission.)*

**The argument we make instead is citation-grounded and needs no new run.** A pruner's source is
the unpruned model, and the pruning literature reports retaining **~96-107%** of unpruned accuracy
at aggressive ratios (RoRA 96.5% at 88.9% pruning on LLaVA-1.5; 106.9% on Qwen2.5-VL at 75%).
Meanwhile:

> **Allocation at 300 tokens (93.7%) exceeds this backbone's own UNPRUNED accuracy at 7957 tokens
> (84.8%) — 110.5% of unpruned, at 1/26th the budget.**

To match it, a pruner would have to exceed its own source by >10%. The best figure we could find
anywhere in the family is 106.9%, on a different backbone and benchmark. **The margin is real but
it is not decisive, and the paper must say so rather than rounding it into a claim.**

**Falsification, restated for this weaker form.** If any pruning method is reported above ~110% of
unpruned on V*Bench with a comparable backbone, this argument fails and the section is cut.

## What this does to §8 (Related Work)

Three families, and we must be precise about which we extend:
1. **Zoom / visual retrieval** (V*, SEAL, ZoomEye, ViCrop, RAP, Chain-of-Spot, Visual CoT) — crop
   and re-encode. **Prior art; we do not claim a better cropper.**
2. **Routing** (ViRGo) — decides *whether* to zoom, via a learned router on an accuracy-vs-**time**
   Pareto axis. **Prior art for the routing idea**; our axis is tokens, not seconds, and we say why.
3. **Pruning** (FastV, D2Pruner, RoRA, HoloV) — selects among encoded tokens. **Not a competitor
   but a contrast**: different axis, and §6b tests whether the axis matters.
